/* Original Python exam study lessons: area 5. */
window.LESSONS.push({"id":"a5-1","area":5,"title":"Python syntax, logic, and runtime errors","objectives":["5.1","5.2","5.3"],"sections":[{"heading":"Parse first, then execute","body":"<p>Python parses source before it can run. Misspelled keywords, missing colons, unmatched parentheses, and invalid indentation prevent execution and produce <code>SyntaxError</code> or its subclass <code>IndentationError</code>. A runtime error occurs only after parsing succeeds: an undefined name raises <code>NameError</code>, an invalid operand combination often raises <code>TypeError</code>, and a failed conversion such as <code>int(\"pear\")</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>.</p><pre><code>if ready:\n    print(\"go\")\nelse:\n    print(\"wait\")</code></pre><p>Indentation marks each suite. Comments begin with <code>#</code> outside strings and continue to the line end.</p>"},{"heading":"Boolean operators return operands","body":"<p><code>not</code> always returns a Boolean. <code>and</code> and <code>or</code> short-circuit and return one of their operands. Empty containers, zero, <code>False</code>, <code>None</code>, and empty strings are falsey; most other values are truthy. Use parentheses where they make compound conditions easier to read.</p><pre><code>name = supplied_name or \"anonymous\"\nsafe = denominator and numerator / denominator</code></pre><p>These expressions return values, which may be useful, but avoid assuming the result of <code>and</code>/<code>or</code> is exactly <code>True</code> or <code>False</code>.</p>"},{"heading":"Recognize common runtime failures","body":"<p>Common distinctions: missing variable → <code>NameError</code>; unsupported operation between types → <code>TypeError</code>; correctly typed but invalid value → <code>ValueError</code>; list position out of range → <code>IndexError</code>; missing dictionary key → <code>KeyError</code>; division by zero → <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>. Trace expressions in evaluation order and use short-circuit behavior to determine which operations actually run.</p><pre><code>values = [10]\n# values[2] raises IndexError\n# int(\"ten\") raises ValueError</code></pre>"}],"sources":[{"label":"Python tutorial: errors and exceptions","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/errors.html"},{"label":"Python expressions: Boolean operations","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#boolean-operations"},{"label":"Python lexical analysis: comments and indentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/reference/lexical_analysis.html"}],"questions":[{"id":"a5s-01","objective":"5.1","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You are reviewing a teammate's program. The variable <code>score</code> has already been assigned an integer value.</p><p>Which statement runs without a syntax error?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["if score >= 10 print(\"pass\")","if score >= 10: print(\"pass\")","if score => 10: print(\"pass\")","if (score >= 10) then: print(\"pass\")"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p>An <code>if</code> header must end with a colon, and the \"greater than or equal\" operator is <code>&gt;=</code>. Python has no <code>then</code> keyword and <code>=&gt;</code> is not an operator. The trap is that syntax errors stop the program before any line executes.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-02","objective":"5.1","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following program.</p><pre><code>01 print(\"start\")\n02 total = (4 + 3\n03 print(total)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["A SyntaxError occurs and nothing is displayed.","The word start is displayed, then a SyntaxError occurs.","The words start and 7 are displayed.","A NameError occurs on line 03."],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>Python parses the whole file before running it. The unclosed parenthesis on line 02 is detected during parsing, so <code>print(\"start\")</code> never executes. A runtime error would let line 01 run first.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-03","objective":"5.1","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You are examining the following code.</p><pre><code>01 print(\"ready\") # print(\"later\")\n02 # print(\"done\")\n03 print(\"# end\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"Line 01 displays <code>ready</code> but not <code>later</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"Line 02 displays <code>done</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"Line 03 displays <code># end</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>A <code>#</code> outside a string starts a comment that runs to the end of the line. Inside the string literal on line 03 it is ordinary text, so it is printed.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-04","objective":"5.1","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 status = False or 0 or \"ok\"\n02 print(status)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["The value True is displayed.","The value False is displayed.","The value 0 is displayed.","The value ok is displayed."],"answer":3}],"explanation":"<p><code>or</code> returns the first truthy operand, not a Boolean. <code>False</code> and <code>0</code> are falsey, so the string <code>\"ok\"</code> is returned.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-05","objective":"5.2","kind":"input","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 a = 3\n02 b = 2\n03 print(not (a &lt; b) and 0)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What value is displayed?</p><p>Enter the number as an integer here:</p>","parts":[{"label":"Enter the number as an integer","input":true,"answer":["0"]}],"explanation":"<p><code>a &lt; b</code> is False, so <code>not</code> yields True. Because the left operand of <code>and</code> is truthy, the right operand is returned, which is <code>0</code>. The trap is expecting a Boolean result.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-06","objective":"5.2","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are writing a program that flags a test score for review. The score must be flagged when it is below 40 or above 90.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"score = 95\nif score {{0}} 40 {{1}} score {{2}} 90:\n    print(\"review\")","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":[">","<","=="],"answer":1},{"label":"Drop-down 2","options":["or","and","not"],"answer":0},{"label":"Drop-down 3","options":[">","<",">="],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>A score is outside the acceptable range when either comparison is true, so the comparisons are joined with <code>or</code>. Using <code>and</code> would require a score both below 40 and above 90, which is impossible.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-07","objective":"5.3","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following program.</p><pre><code>01 total = 0\n02 for n in [1, 2, 3]:\n03     total += n\n04 print(totl)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is causing the error?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["Line 03 adds an integer to an integer.","Line 02 loops over a list of integers.","Line 04 refers to a name that has not been defined.","Line 01 assigns an integer instead of a float."],"answer":2}],"explanation":"<p>The misspelled name <code>totl</code> was never assigned, so evaluating it raises <code>NameError</code> at runtime. The earlier lines are valid and run normally before the error occurs.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-08","objective":"5.3","kind":"drag","prompt":"<p>You are debugging a script. Each statement below is run in a new Python session in which <code>age</code> was never assigned.</p><p>Move the appropriate exception from the list on the left to the correct statement on the right. You may use each exception once, more than once, or not at all.</p>","bank":["NameError","TypeError","ValueError","KeyError","IndexError"],"parts":[{"label":"<code>print(\"age\" + 3)</code>","options":["NameError","TypeError","ValueError","KeyError","IndexError"],"answer":1},{"label":"<code>print(age)</code>","options":["NameError","TypeError","ValueError","KeyError","IndexError"],"answer":0},{"label":"<code>int(\"3.5\")</code>","options":["NameError","TypeError","ValueError","KeyError","IndexError"],"answer":2}],"explanation":"<p>Adding a <code>str</code> and an <code>int</code> is an unsupported operand combination (<code>TypeError</code>). An unbound name is a <code>NameError</code>. <code>int(\"3.5\")</code> receives the right type but an invalid value, which is <code>ValueError</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-09","objective":"5.3","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 raw = \"three\"\n02 number = int(raw)\n03 print(number)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["A TypeError occurs.","A ValueError occurs.","A SyntaxError occurs.","The value 3 is displayed."],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p><code>int()</code> accepts a string, so the type is acceptable, but the text cannot be converted to a number. A correct type with an invalid value raises <code>ValueError</code>, not <code>TypeError</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-10","objective":"5.3","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 items = [1]\n02 print(items[0])\n03 print(items[2])\n04 print(items[-1])</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"Line 02 raises an <code>IndexError</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"Line 03 raises an <code>IndexError</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"Line 04 is executed.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p>Index 0 is valid, so line 02 displays 1. Index 2 is out of range for a one-item list, so line 03 raises <code>IndexError</code> and the program stops; line 04 never runs (and <code>items[-1]</code> would have been valid).</p>"},{"id":"a5s-11","objective":"5.3","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are writing a program that must display a message instead of stopping when a division by zero occurs.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"try:\n    ratio = 1 / 0\n{{0}} {{1}}:\n    print(\"cannot divide\")","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":["catch","finally","except"],"answer":2},{"label":"Drop-down 2","options":["ValueError","ZeroDivisionError","TypeError"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p>Python handles exceptions with <code>except</code>, not <code>catch</code>. Dividing by zero raises <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>; an except clause for a different type would not match and the program would still fail.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-12","objective":"5.2","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 items = []\n02 if items:\n03     print(\"A\")\n04 else:\n05     print(\"B\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["The value B is displayed.","The value A is displayed.","The values A and B are displayed.","A TypeError occurs because a list cannot be a condition."],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>An empty list is falsey in a Boolean context, so the <code>if</code> suite is skipped and the <code>else</code> suite runs. Any object can be used as a condition.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-13","objective":"5.1","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are writing a multi-branch conditional that displays a description of the temperature.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"temp = 35\nif temp > 30{{0}}\n    print(\"hot\")\n{{1}} temp > 20:\n    print(\"warm\")\n{{2}}:\n    print(\"mild\")","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":[":",";","then"],"answer":0},{"label":"Drop-down 2","options":["elif","else if","elseif"],"answer":0},{"label":"Drop-down 3","options":["otherwise","default","else"],"answer":2}],"explanation":"<p>Each compound-statement header ends with a colon, the middle branch uses the single keyword <code>elif</code>, and the final fallback is <code>else</code>. Python has no <code>then</code>, <code>else if</code> or <code>default</code> syntax.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-14","objective":"5.2","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 print(\"\" and 9)\n02 print(0 or \"\")\n03 print(3 and 9)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"Line 01 displays an empty line.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"Line 02 displays <code>0</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"Line 03 displays <code>9</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p><code>and</code> returns the first falsey operand, or the last operand if all are truthy. Line 01 returns <code>\"\"</code>, line 03 returns <code>9</code>. <code>or</code> on line 02 returns the last operand when none are truthy, which is <code>\"\"</code>, not <code>0</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-15","objective":"5.1","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following program.</p><pre><code>01 def f():\n02 print(\"x\")\n03 f()</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["The value x is displayed.","A NameError occurs because f is not defined.","A TypeError occurs when f is called.","An IndentationError occurs."],"answer":3}],"explanation":"<p>A <code>def</code> header requires an indented suite. The missing body is reported while parsing as <code>IndentationError</code> (a subclass of <code>SyntaxError</code>), so nothing runs.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-16","objective":"5.3","kind":"drag","prompt":"<p>You need to predict the result of several string operations.</p><p>Move the appropriate result from the list on the left to the correct statement on the right. You may use each result once, more than once, or not at all.</p>","bank":["An empty line is displayed","xx is displayed","TypeError","ValueError","ZeroDivisionError"],"parts":[{"label":"<code>print(\"x\" * -2)</code>","options":["An empty line is displayed","xx is displayed","TypeError","ValueError","ZeroDivisionError"],"answer":0},{"label":"<code>print(\"x\" * 2)</code>","options":["An empty line is displayed","xx is displayed","TypeError","ValueError","ZeroDivisionError"],"answer":1},{"label":"<code>print(\"x\" * \"2\")</code>","options":["An empty line is displayed","xx is displayed","TypeError","ValueError","ZeroDivisionError"],"answer":2}],"explanation":"<p>Repeating a string by a negative integer is allowed and yields an empty string, so no exception is raised. Repeating by a positive integer concatenates copies, while a string multiplier is an unsupported operand type.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-17","objective":"5.2","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are writing code with the following requirements:</p><ul><li><code>result</code> must be <code>\"fallback\"</code> when <code>n</code> is 0.</li><li><code>safe</code> must be 0 when <code>n</code> is 0, without raising <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>.</li></ul><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"n = 0\nresult = n {{0}} \"fallback\"\nsafe = n {{1}} 100 / n\nprint(result)\nprint(safe)","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":["and","or","not"],"answer":1},{"label":"Drop-down 2","options":["and","or","+"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p><code>0 or \"fallback\"</code> skips the falsey 0 and returns the string. <code>0 and 100 / n</code> short-circuits and returns 0 without evaluating the division; <code>or</code> or <code>+</code> would evaluate <code>100 / n</code> and raise.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-18","objective":"5.1","kind":"multi","prompt":"<p>You are writing a program that displays an alert when the variable <code>t</code> is below 10 or above 35.</p><p>Which two conditions should you use? (Choose 2.) Each correct answer presents a complete solution.</p>","parts":[{"label":"Choose two.","options":["t &gt; 35 and t &lt; 10","t &lt; 10 or t &gt; 35","10 &gt; t &gt; 35","t &lt; 10 or &gt; 35","not (10 &lt;= t &lt;= 35)"],"answer":[1,4],"multi":true}],"explanation":"<p>The alert is needed when either comparison is true, so <code>or</code> (or the negation of the acceptable range) works. <code>and</code> and the chained comparison <code>10 &gt; t &gt; 35</code> can never be true, and <code>or &gt; 35</code> is a syntax error.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-19","objective":"5.3","kind":"drag","prompt":"<p>A program contains the following expressions. Each is run on its own.</p><p>Move the appropriate exception from the list on the left to the expression that raises it on the right. You may use each exception once, more than once, or not at all.</p>","bank":["ZeroDivisionError","KeyError","NameError","IndexError","ValueError"],"parts":[{"label":"<code>{}[\"missing\"]</code>","options":["ZeroDivisionError","KeyError","NameError","IndexError","ValueError"],"answer":1},{"label":"<code>[1, 2][5]</code>","options":["ZeroDivisionError","KeyError","NameError","IndexError","ValueError"],"answer":3},{"label":"<code>10 // 0</code>","options":["ZeroDivisionError","KeyError","NameError","IndexError","ValueError"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>A missing dictionary key raises <code>KeyError</code>; an out-of-range list position raises <code>IndexError</code>; integer floor division by zero raises <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>. Lookups on different container types raise different exceptions.</p>"},{"id":"a5s-20","objective":"5.3","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 numerator = 8\n02 denominator = 0\n03 if denominator and numerator / denominator &gt; 2:\n04     print(\"large\")\n05 else:\n06     print(\"safe\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"Line 03 raises a <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"The value <code>safe</code> is displayed.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"If <code>and</code> on line 03 were changed to <code>or</code>, a <code>ZeroDivisionError</code> would occur.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>Because <code>denominator</code> is 0 (falsey), <code>and</code> short-circuits and the division is never evaluated, so the else suite runs. With <code>or</code> the right operand must be evaluated and the division raises.</p>"}]});
window.LESSONS.push({"id":"a5-2","area":5,"title":"Exception flow and unittest assertions","objectives":["5.4","5.5"],"sections":[{"heading":"Control flow with try, except, else, finally","body":"<p>Put statements that may fail in <code>try</code>. A matching <code>except</code> handles the exception. The optional <code>else</code> suite runs only if the try suite completed without an exception. The optional <code>finally</code> suite runs while leaving the construct, whether execution succeeded or an exception is propagating, and is commonly used for cleanup.</p><pre><code>try:\n    amount = int(text)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"enter a whole number\")\nelse:\n    print(amount)\nfinally:\n    print(\"finished\")</code></pre><p>Prefer narrow exception types; broad handlers can conceal programming errors. Put success-only work in <code>else</code>, outside the protected try suite.</p>"},{"heading":"Raise and preserve useful context","body":"<p>Use <code>raise ValueError(\"...\")</code> to signal invalid values. A bare <code>raise</code> inside an active handler reraises the same exception and preserves its traceback. Exception classes may be subclassed; list specific subclasses before broader base classes in handlers. <code>raise NewError(...) from err</code> records an explicit cause when translating an error.</p><pre><code>try:\n    count = int(text)\nexcept ValueError as err:\n    raise ValueError(\"count must be an integer\") from err</code></pre><p>Be cautious about <code>return</code> or raising another error inside <code>finally</code>, because it can replace or alter a pending return or exception.</p>"},{"heading":"Write focused unittest checks","body":"<p>Subclass <code>unittest.TestCase</code> and define methods named <code>test_...</code>. Assertions fail by raising a failure that the test runner reports. Pick the assertion that matches the contract: <code>assertEqual(a, b)</code> for values, <code>assertTrue(expr)</code> for truthiness, <code>assertIs(a, b)</code> for identity, <code>assertIn(member, container)</code> for membership, and <code>assertIsInstance(obj, cls)</code> for class/subclass membership.</p><pre><code>import unittest\n\nclass TestLookup(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_default(self):\n        result = lookup(\"missing\")\n        self.assertEqual(result, [])\n        self.assertIsInstance(result, list)</code></pre><p>Do not use truthiness when an empty value is valid, or identity when equality is intended.</p>"}],"sources":[{"label":"Python tutorial: errors and exceptions","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/errors.html"},{"label":"unittest — Unit testing framework","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/unittest.html"},{"label":"unittest: TestCase assertions","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/unittest.html#testcase-assertions"}],"questions":[{"id":"a5e-01","objective":"5.4","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 try:\n02     print(\"A\")\n03     value = int(\"x\")\n04     print(\"B\")\n05 except ValueError:\n06     print(\"C\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["The values A, B, and C are displayed.","The value A is displayed, then a ValueError occurs.","The values A and C are displayed.","The value C is displayed."],"answer":2}],"explanation":"<p>Statements run in order until one raises. Line 03 raises <code>ValueError</code>, so line 04 is skipped and control jumps to the matching except clause. Line 02 already ran, so A is displayed first.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-02","objective":"5.4","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 try:\n02     n = int(\"12\")\n03 except ValueError:\n04     print(\"bad\")\n05 else:\n06     print(\"ok\")\n07 finally:\n08     print(\"done\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"The statement on line 04 executes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"The statement on line 06 executes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"The statement on line 08 executes after line 06.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>The conversion on line 02 succeeds, so no exception is raised: the except suite is skipped and the else suite runs. The finally suite always runs last.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-03","objective":"5.4","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You are writing a Python program that opens a network connection inside a <code>try</code> statement. The connection must be released whether or not an exception occurs.</p><p>Which clause should contain the release code?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["else","finally","except Exception","try"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p>The <code>finally</code> clause runs on every exit path from the statement: success, handled exception, or exception propagating. The <code>else</code> clause runs only if no exception was raised, so cleanup there would be skipped on failure.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-04","objective":"5.4","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are writing code that logs a message when a conversion fails. After logging, the original <code>ValueError</code> must continue to propagate to the caller unchanged.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"try:\n    value = int(\"x\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"logging\")\n    {{0}}\nprint(\"after\")","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":["raise","pass","return None"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>A bare <code>raise</code> inside an except suite re-raises the active exception with its original traceback. <code>pass</code> would swallow the error and let \"after\" print.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-05","objective":"5.4","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are writing a function that rejects invalid input. The function must raise a built-in <code>ValueError</code> with a descriptive message when <code>age</code> is negative.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"def set_age(age):\n    if age < 0:\n        {{0}} {{1}}(\"Age cannot be negative\")\n    return age","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":["throw","except","raise"],"answer":2},{"label":"Drop-down 2","options":["ValueException","ValueError","InvalidValue"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p>Python signals errors with the <code>raise</code> statement; there is no <code>throw</code>. The built-in class is named <code>ValueError</code>, and the other names do not exist, which would produce a <code>NameError</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-06","objective":"5.4","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>The file <code>missing.txt</code> does not exist. You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 try:\n02     open(\"missing.txt\")\n03 except OSError:\n04     print(\"os\")\n05 except FileNotFoundError:\n06     print(\"fnf\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["The value os is displayed.","The value fnf is displayed.","The values os and fnf are displayed.","A SyntaxError occurs because the handlers are in the wrong order."],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p><code>FileNotFoundError</code> is a subclass of <code>OSError</code>. Handlers are tried top to bottom and the first match wins, so the broad clause on line 03 catches it and the specific clause is unreachable. List specific exceptions first.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-07","objective":"5.4","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 def parse(v):\n02     try:\n03         return int(v)\n04     except (ValueError, TypeError):\n05         return -1\n06 \n07 print(parse(\"x\"), parse(None))\n08 print({}[\"k\"])</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"<code>parse(\"x\")</code> returns -1.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"<code>parse(None)</code> raises a <code>TypeError</code> that is not handled.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"A <code>KeyError</code> raised inside the try suite would be handled by line 04.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p>A tuple lets one except clause handle several types. <code>\"x\"</code> causes <code>ValueError</code> and <code>None</code> causes <code>TypeError</code>; both return -1. <code>KeyError</code> is not in the tuple, so it would propagate.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-08","objective":"5.4","kind":"drag","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 try:\n02     print(\"T1\")\n03     int(\"bad\")\n04     print(\"T2\")\n05 except ValueError:\n06     print(\"E\")\n07 finally:\n08     print(\"F\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>Move the appropriate value from the list on the left to the correct position in the output on the right. You may use each value once, more than once, or not at all.</p>","bank":["T2","F","T1","E"],"parts":[{"label":"First line displayed","options":["T2","F","T1","E"],"answer":2},{"label":"Second line displayed","options":["T2","F","T1","E"],"answer":3},{"label":"Third line displayed","options":["T2","F","T1","E"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p><code>T1</code> prints before the failing conversion. The exception skips <code>T2</code>, the except suite prints <code>E</code>, and the finally suite always runs last.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-09","objective":"5.4","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 try:\n02     print(\"try\")\n03 except ValueError:\n04     print(\"except\")\n05 else:\n06     print(\"else\")\n07 finally:\n08     print(\"finally\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["The words try and finally are displayed.","The words try, except, and finally are displayed.","The words try, except, else, and finally are displayed.","The words try, else, and finally are displayed in that order."],"answer":3}],"explanation":"<p>When the try suite completes without an exception, the except suites are skipped, the else suite runs, and finally runs last. The trap is forgetting that <code>else</code> exists for the success path.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-10","objective":"5.4","kind":"input","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 def f():\n02     try:\n03         return 1\n04     finally:\n05         return 2\n06 \n07 print(f())</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What value is displayed?</p><p>Enter the number as an integer here:</p>","parts":[{"label":"Enter the number as an integer","input":true,"answer":["2"]}],"explanation":"<p>The finally suite runs before the function actually returns. A <code>return</code> there replaces the pending <code>return 1</code>, so the function returns 2. Returning from finally is discouraged because it can hide exceptions.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-11","objective":"5.4","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>A teammate wraps a long-running loop in a <code>try</code> statement that ends with a bare <code>except:</code> clause. You are asked to review it.</p><p>What is the problem with the bare <code>except:</code>?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["It only catches exceptions that inherit from ValueError.","It causes a SyntaxError in Python 3.","It catches every exception, including KeyboardInterrupt and SystemExit, and can hide real bugs.","It prevents the finally clause from running."],"answer":2}],"explanation":"<p>A bare <code>except:</code> catches all exceptions, even ones such as <code>KeyboardInterrupt</code> that should normally end the program. Catch specific types, or at most <code>except Exception</code>, so unexpected errors are not silenced.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-12","objective":"5.4","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are writing a function that converts a low-level <code>ValueError</code> into a <code>RuntimeError</code> while keeping the original error as its explicit cause. The caller then displays the type of the cause.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"def run():\n    try:\n        int(\"x\")\n    except ValueError as err:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"config invalid\") {{0}} err\n\ntry:\n    run()\nexcept RuntimeError as exc:\n    print(type(exc.{{1}}).__name__)","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":["from","as","with"],"answer":0},{"label":"Drop-down 2","options":["__cause__","__origin__","__source__"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p><code>raise X from err</code> chains the exceptions and stores the original in the <code>__cause__</code> attribute. The other attribute names do not exist, and <code>as</code> is not valid in a raise statement.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-13","objective":"5.4","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 try:\n02     raise ValueError(\"first\")\n03 finally:\n04     raise KeyError(\"second\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>Which exception does the program end with?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["A ValueError occurs.","A KeyError occurs.","Both exceptions are raised separately and the program continues.","No exception occurs because finally handles the ValueError."],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p>The finally suite runs while the <code>ValueError</code> is propagating. Raising a new exception there replaces it as the active exception, so the program ends with <code>KeyError</code> (the traceback shows the ValueError only as context).</p>"},{"id":"a5e-14","objective":"5.4","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 try:\n02     int(\"x\")\n03 except Exception as exc:\n04     print(type(exc).__name__)\n05     print(exc.args[0])\n06 print(exc)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"Line 04 displays <code>ValueError</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"Line 05 displays <code>ValueError</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"Line 06 raises a <code>NameError</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p><code>exc</code> is bound to the exception instance, so <code>type(exc).__name__</code> is the class name while <code>exc.args[0]</code> is the message text. Python deletes the name when the except block ends, so line 06 raises <code>NameError</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-15","objective":"5.4","kind":"multi","prompt":"<p>You are writing a function that receives a negative <code>rate</code> value. The function must signal an invalid argument so that callers can handle it with <code>try/except ValueError</code>.</p><p>Which two statements should you use? (Choose 2.) Each correct answer presents a complete solution.</p>","parts":[{"label":"Choose two.","options":["print(\"bad rate\")","raise ValueError(\"rate must be positive\")","return \"error\"","except ValueError:","raise ValueError(f\"bad rate: {rate}\")"],"answer":[1,4],"multi":true}],"explanation":"<p>Callers can only catch an exception that is raised. <code>raise ValueError(...)</code> works with either a literal or an f-string message. Returning an error string or printing leaves the caller with no exception to handle.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-16","objective":"5.4","kind":"drag","prompt":"<p>You are writing a function that must meet these requirements:</p><ul><li>It converts text to an integer.</li><li>It displays <code>invalid</code> if the conversion fails.</li><li>It displays <code>parsed</code> only if the conversion succeeds.</li><li>It displays <code>closed</code> in every case.</li></ul><p>Complete the code by moving the appropriate code segments from the list on the left to the correct locations on the right. You may use each code segment once, more than once, or not at all.</p>","bank":["print(\"parsed\")","print(\"closed\")","value = int(text)","print(\"invalid\")"],"parts":[{"label":"<code>try:</code> block","options":["print(\"parsed\")","print(\"closed\")","value = int(text)","print(\"invalid\")"],"answer":2},{"label":"<code>except ValueError:</code> block","options":["print(\"parsed\")","print(\"closed\")","value = int(text)","print(\"invalid\")"],"answer":3},{"label":"<code>else:</code> block","options":["print(\"parsed\")","print(\"closed\")","value = int(text)","print(\"invalid\")"],"answer":0},{"label":"<code>finally:</code> block","options":["print(\"parsed\")","print(\"closed\")","value = int(text)","print(\"invalid\")"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p>Only the risky conversion belongs in <code>try</code>. The success-only message goes in <code>else</code>, which runs only when no exception occurred, and the unconditional message goes in <code>finally</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-17","objective":"5.4","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 try:\n02     [1][5]\n03 except LookupError:\n04     print(\"lookup\")\n05 try:\n06     {}[\"k\"]\n07 except IndexError:\n08     print(\"index\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"Line 04 executes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"Line 08 executes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"The program ends with an unhandled <code>KeyError</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p><code>IndexError</code> and <code>KeyError</code> both inherit from <code>LookupError</code>, so the first handler catches the list error. The second handler names only <code>IndexError</code>, which does not match a <code>KeyError</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-18","objective":"5.4","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 try:\n02     raise ValueError\n03 except ValueError as err:\n04     print(len(err.args))</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["The value 0 is displayed.","The value 1 is displayed.","A TypeError occurs because ValueError was not called.","A SyntaxError occurs because parentheses are required."],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>When <code>raise</code> is given an exception class, Python instantiates it with no arguments, as if you wrote <code>ValueError()</code>. The exception is caught normally and its <code>args</code> tuple is empty.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-19","objective":"5.4","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are writing code that writes to a file. The file must be closed automatically whether or not the write raises an exception.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"{{0}} open(\"data.txt\", \"w\") {{1}} handle:\n    handle.write(\"ok\")\nprint(handle.closed)","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":["try","using","with"],"answer":2},{"label":"Drop-down 2","options":["in","as","from"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p>A <code>with</code> statement calls the file object's cleanup on exit, even when an exception occurs, and <code>as</code> binds the object to a name. <code>using</code> is not a Python keyword.</p>"},{"id":"a5e-20","objective":"5.4","kind":"drag","prompt":"<p>You run the following code.</p><pre><code>01 try:\n02     print(\"A\")\n03     raise KeyError\n04 except ValueError:\n05     print(\"B\")\n06 finally:\n07     print(\"C\")\n08 print(\"D\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>Move the appropriate value from the list on the left to the correct position on the right. You may use each value once, more than once, or not at all.</p>","bank":["B","D","KeyError","C","A"],"parts":[{"label":"First line displayed","options":["B","D","KeyError","C","A"],"answer":4},{"label":"Second line displayed","options":["B","D","KeyError","C","A"],"answer":3},{"label":"Final line of the traceback","options":["B","D","KeyError","C","A"],"answer":2}],"explanation":"<p>No except clause matches <code>KeyError</code>, so <code>B</code> is skipped. The finally suite still runs, printing C, and the exception then keeps propagating and ends the program, so <code>D</code> never prints.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-01","objective":"5.5","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are writing a unit test for a function that adds numbers.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"import unittest\n\nclass TestTotals({{0}}.{{1}}):\n    def test_sum(self):\n        self.assertEqual(sum([1, 2]), 3)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":["unittest","test","TestCase"],"answer":0},{"label":"Drop-down 2","options":["TestCase","Test","Case"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>Test classes inherit from <code>unittest.TestCase</code>. The base class lives in the <code>unittest</code> module and has that exact name; other names raise <code>AttributeError</code> or <code>NameError</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-02","objective":"5.5","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run <code>python -m unittest</code> on a module that contains the following class.</p><pre><code>01 class TestMath(unittest.TestCase):\n02     def test_add(self):\n03         self.assertEqual(1 + 1, 2)\n04     def add_test(self):\n05         self.assertEqual(1 + 1, 2)\n06     def check_add(self):\n07         self.assertEqual(1 + 1, 2)\n08     def run_add(self):\n09         self.assertEqual(1 + 1, 2)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["The output reports that 2 tests ran.","The output reports that 3 tests ran.","The output reports that 4 tests ran.","The output reports that 1 test ran."],"answer":3}],"explanation":"<p>The default loader only collects methods whose names begin with <code>test</code>. The other three methods are ordinary helpers and are never run automatically.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-03","objective":"5.5","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following test.</p><pre><code>01 class TestMath(unittest.TestCase):\n02     def test_math(self):\n03         self.assertEqual(2 + 2, 5)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["The test passes.","The test reports an error and the summary reports FAILED (errors=1).","The test fails and the summary reports FAILED (failures=1).","The test is skipped."],"answer":2}],"explanation":"<p><code>assertEqual(a, b)</code> passes only when <code>a == b</code>. Here <code>4 != 5</code>, so it raises <code>AssertionError</code>, which unittest counts as a failure rather than an error.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-04","objective":"5.5","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You run the following tests.</p><pre><code>01 class TestTruth(unittest.TestCase):\n02     def test_a(self):\n03         self.assertTrue(\"hello\")\n04     def test_b(self):\n05         self.assertTrue(\"\")\n06     def test_c(self):\n07         self.assertTrue(1)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"<code>test_a</code> passes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"<code>test_b</code> passes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"<code>assertTrue</code> requires its argument to be exactly the Boolean <code>True</code>.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p><code>assertTrue(x)</code> checks <code>bool(x)</code>, so any truthy value such as a non-empty string passes, and the empty string fails. It does not require the value to be the <code>True</code> object.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-05","objective":"5.5","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are testing list handling. The tests must meet these requirements:</p><ul><li><code>test_same_object</code> verifies that <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are the same object.</li><li><code>test_equal_items</code> verifies that <code>a</code> and <code>c</code> contain equal items.</li><li><code>test_different_object</code> verifies that <code>a</code> and <code>c</code> are not the same object.</li></ul><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"a = []\nb = a\nc = []\n\nclass TestLists(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_same_object(self):\n        self.{{0}}(a, b)\n    def test_equal_items(self):\n        self.{{1}}(a, c)\n    def test_different_object(self):\n        self.{{2}}(a, c)","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":["assertIsNot","assertNotEqual","assertIs"],"answer":2},{"label":"Drop-down 2","options":["assertIs","assertEqual","assertNotEqual"],"answer":1},{"label":"Drop-down 3","options":["assertIsNot","assertIs","assertNotEqual"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p><code>assertIs</code> and <code>assertIsNot</code> test identity (<code>is</code>), while <code>assertEqual</code> tests value equality (<code>==</code>). Two separately created empty lists are equal but not identical.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-06","objective":"5.5","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are writing tests that must meet these requirements:</p><ul><li><code>test_list</code> verifies that 3 is an element of the list.</li><li><code>test_dict_key</code> verifies that 7 is not a key in the dictionary.</li></ul><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"class TestMembership(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_list(self):\n        self.{{0}}(3, [1, 2, 3])\n    def test_dict_key(self):\n        self.{{1}}(7, {\"id\": 7})","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":["assertIn","assertNotIn","assertEqual"],"answer":0},{"label":"Drop-down 2","options":["assertIn","assertIsNone","assertNotIn"],"answer":2}],"explanation":"<p><code>assertIn(member, container)</code> checks membership. For a dictionary, membership tests keys, not values, so 7 is not \"in\" <code>{\"id\": 7}</code> and <code>assertNotIn</code> passes.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-07","objective":"5.5","kind":"drag","prompt":"<p>You are writing unit tests for several functions.</p><p>Move the appropriate assertion method from the list on the left to the correct check on the right. You may use each method once, more than once, or not at all.</p>","bank":["assertIn","assertIsInstance","assertTrue","assertEqual","assertIsNone","assertIs"],"parts":[{"label":"The result equals <code>\"done\"</code>","options":["assertIn","assertIsInstance","assertTrue","assertEqual","assertIsNone","assertIs"],"answer":3},{"label":"The result is <code>None</code>","options":["assertIn","assertIsInstance","assertTrue","assertEqual","assertIsNone","assertIs"],"answer":4},{"label":"The result is a <code>list</code>","options":["assertIn","assertIsInstance","assertTrue","assertEqual","assertIsNone","assertIs"],"answer":1},{"label":"The text <code>\"on\"</code> appears inside the result","options":["assertIn","assertIsInstance","assertTrue","assertEqual","assertIsNone","assertIs"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>Choose the assertion that states the intent: value equality uses <code>assertEqual</code>, the None singleton uses <code>assertIsNone</code>, a type check uses <code>assertIsInstance</code>, and substring or element membership uses <code>assertIn</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-08","objective":"5.5","kind":"drag","prompt":"<p>You are completing a test module for a function named <code>double</code>.</p><pre><code>class TestMath(<b>[1]</b>):\n    def <b>[2]</b>(self):\n        <b>[3]</b>.assertEqual(double(4), 8)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.<b>[4]</b>()</code></pre><p>Complete the code by moving the appropriate code segments from the list on the left to the correct locations on the right. You may use each code segment once, more than once, or not at all.</p>","bank":["unittest.TestCase","double_test","self","main","test_double","unittest"],"parts":[{"label":"[1] Base class","options":["unittest.TestCase","double_test","self","main","test_double","unittest"],"answer":0},{"label":"[2] Test method name","options":["unittest.TestCase","double_test","self","main","test_double","unittest"],"answer":4},{"label":"[3] Object that provides the assertion","options":["unittest.TestCase","double_test","self","main","test_double","unittest"],"answer":2},{"label":"[4] Function that runs the tests","options":["unittest.TestCase","double_test","self","main","test_double","unittest"],"answer":3}],"explanation":"<p>Test classes extend <code>unittest.TestCase</code>, test methods must start with <code>test</code>, assertions are inherited methods called on <code>self</code>, and <code>unittest.main()</code> discovers and runs the tests.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-09","objective":"5.5","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You run the following tests.</p><pre><code>01 a = [1, 2]\n02 b = [1, 2]\n03 \n04 class TestLists(unittest.TestCase):\n05     def test_eq(self):\n06         self.assertEqual(a, b)\n07     def test_is(self):\n08         self.assertIs(a, b)\n09     def test_is_not(self):\n10         self.assertIsNot(a, b)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"<code>test_eq</code> passes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"<code>test_is</code> passes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"<code>test_is_not</code> fails because the lists contain equal items.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p>Two separately created lists with the same items are equal, so <code>assertEqual</code> passes. They are different objects, so <code>assertIs</code> fails and <code>assertIsNot</code> passes. Equality and identity are different checks.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-10","objective":"5.5","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>A function <code>find_user()</code> returns <code>None</code> when no user matches. You are writing a test for the no-match case.</p><p>Which is the best reason to use <code>self.assertIsNone(result)</code> instead of <code>self.assertEqual(result, None)</code>?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["It also passes when result is 0 or an empty list.","It states the intent directly, checks identity with None, and reports a clearer failure message.","It is the only assertion that can compare against None without raising an error.","It makes the test method run before other tests."],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p><code>assertIsNone</code> uses an <code>is None</code> check and produces a message such as \"0 is not None\". It does not pass for other falsey values. Both forms can compare to <code>None</code>, so the benefit is clarity.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-11","objective":"5.5","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following tests.</p><pre><code>01 class TestRun(unittest.TestCase):\n02     def test_a(self):\n03         self.assertEqual(1, 2)\n04     def test_b(self):\n05         self.assertEqual(2, 2)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["Both tests run and one failure is reported.","Only test_a runs because the first failure stops the run.","Both tests run and one error is reported.","Both tests run and are reported as passed."],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>A failed assertion raises <code>AssertionError</code>, which fails only that test method; unittest then continues with the other tests. It is counted as a failure, not an error (errors are other unexpected exceptions).</p>"},{"id":"a5t-12","objective":"5.5","kind":"drag","prompt":"<p>You are writing assertions for the list <code>values = [1, 2, 3]</code>.</p><p>Move the appropriate assertion method from the list on the left to the correct check on the right. You may use each method once, more than once, or not at all.</p>","bank":["assertEqual","assertIn","assertIs","assertNotIn"],"parts":[{"label":"3 is an element of <code>values</code>","options":["assertEqual","assertIn","assertIs","assertNotIn"],"answer":1},{"label":"7 is not an element of <code>values</code>","options":["assertEqual","assertIn","assertIs","assertNotIn"],"answer":3},{"label":"<code>len(values)</code> equals 3","options":["assertEqual","assertIn","assertIs","assertNotIn"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>Element membership is tested with <code>assertIn</code> and its negation <code>assertNotIn</code>. Comparing a computed length to a number is a value comparison, so use <code>assertEqual</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-13","objective":"5.5","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>The function <code>run_job()</code> returns an empty list when it succeeds. You run the following test.</p><pre><code>01 def run_job():\n02     return []\n03 \n04 class TestJob(unittest.TestCase):\n05     def test_job(self):\n06         self.assertTrue(run_job())</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["The test passes because run_job returned a list.","The test reports an error because assertTrue cannot accept a list.","The test is skipped because the result is empty.","The test fails because an empty list is falsey."],"answer":3}],"explanation":"<p><code>assertTrue</code> checks truthiness, and an empty list is falsey. The test should assert the exact expected value, for example <code>assertEqual(run_job(), [])</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-14","objective":"5.5","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You run the following tests.</p><pre><code>01 class Animal:\n02     pass\n03 class Dog(Animal):\n04     pass\n05 \n06 class TestTypes(unittest.TestCase):\n07     def test_a(self):\n08         self.assertIsInstance(Dog(), Animal)\n09     def test_b(self):\n10         self.assertIs(type(Dog()), Animal)\n11     def test_c(self):\n12         self.assertIsInstance(Animal(), Dog)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"<code>test_a</code> passes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"<code>test_b</code> passes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"<code>test_c</code> passes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p><code>assertIsInstance</code> accepts instances of subclasses, so a <code>Dog</code> is an <code>Animal</code>. The exact type of a <code>Dog</code> is <code>Dog</code>, and a plain <code>Animal</code> is not a <code>Dog</code>.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-15","objective":"5.5","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You run the following tests.</p><pre><code>01 class TestValue(unittest.TestCase):\n02     def setup(self):\n03         self.value = 5\n04     def test_value(self):\n05         self.assertEqual(self.value, 5)</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>What is the result?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["The test passes.","The test fails with an AssertionError because the value is not 5.","The test reports an error because self.value does not exist.","A SyntaxError occurs because setup is a reserved name."],"answer":2}],"explanation":"<p>unittest calls the hook named exactly <code>setUp</code> (capital U) before each test. A method named <code>setup</code> is never called, so <code>self.value</code> is missing and the test raises <code>AttributeError</code>, which is counted as an error.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-16","objective":"5.5","kind":"dropdown","prompt":"<p>You are writing a test that must verify that <code>finish()</code> returns exactly the string <code>\"done\"</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>","template":"def finish():\n    return \"done\"\n\nclass TestFinish(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_finish(self):\n        {{0}}.{{1}}(finish(), \"done\")","parts":[{"label":"Drop-down 1","options":["unittest","self","TestCase"],"answer":1},{"label":"Drop-down 2","options":["assertEqual","assertNotEqual","assertIsNone"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>Assertion methods are inherited by the test class, so they are called on <code>self</code>. <code>assertEqual</code> compares the returned value with the expected one.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-17","objective":"5.5","kind":"tf","prompt":"<p>You run the following tests.</p><pre><code>01 a = []\n02 b = a\n03 \n04 class TestAlias(unittest.TestCase):\n05     def test_a(self):\n06         self.assertIs(a, b)\n07     def test_b(self):\n08         self.assertIsNot(a, b)\n09     def test_c(self):\n10         self.assertEqual(a, [])</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p><p>Note: You will receive partial credit for each correct selection.</p>","parts":[{"label":"<code>test_a</code> passes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0},{"label":"<code>test_b</code> passes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1},{"label":"<code>test_c</code> passes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0}],"explanation":"<p>The assignment <code>b = a</code> creates a second name for the same list, so <code>assertIs</code> passes and <code>assertIsNot</code> fails. The list is empty, so it equals a new empty list.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-18","objective":"5.5","kind":"input","prompt":"<p>You run the following tests.</p><pre><code>01 class TestText(unittest.TestCase):\n02     def test_1(self):\n03         self.assertIn(\"py\", \"python\")\n04     def test_2(self):\n05         self.assertIn(\"Py\", \"python\")\n06     def test_3(self):\n07         self.assertIn(\"ho\", \"python\")\n08     def test_4(self):\n09         self.assertIn(\"pn\", \"python\")\n10     def test_5(self):\n11         self.assertIn(\"\", \"python\")</code></pre><p>Line numbers are included for reference only.</p><p>How many of the tests pass?</p><p>Enter the number as an integer here:</p>","parts":[{"label":"Enter the number as an integer","input":true,"answer":["3"]}],"explanation":"<p>With strings, <code>assertIn</code> checks for a contiguous, case-sensitive substring. <code>\"py\"</code>, <code>\"ho\"</code>, and the empty string are substrings; <code>\"Py\"</code> differs by case and <code>\"pn\"</code> is not contiguous, so 3 of 5 pass.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-19","objective":"5.5","kind":"mc","prompt":"<p>You have written tests in a file named <code>test_orders.py</code>. You are in the same folder in a terminal.</p><p>Which command runs the tests in the module?</p>","parts":[{"label":"Select one answer","options":["python unittest test_orders","python -m unittest test_orders","python -m test_orders.unittest","unittest run test_orders.py"],"answer":1}],"explanation":"<p>The <code>-m</code> option runs the standard-library <code>unittest</code> module as a script, and the module name follows it. Without <code>-m</code>, Python looks for a file named <code>unittest</code> and fails.</p>"},{"id":"a5t-20","objective":"5.5","kind":"multi","prompt":"<p>A function returns the integer <code>10</code>, stored in <code>result</code>. A test must verify both the type and the exact value of the result.</p><p>Which two assertions should you use together? (Choose 2.)</p>","parts":[{"label":"Choose two.","options":["self.assertIsNone(result)","self.assertIsInstance(result, int)","self.assertTrue(result)","self.assertEqual(result, 10)","self.assertIn(result, \"10\")"],"answer":[1,3],"multi":true}],"explanation":"<p>Type and value are separate properties. <code>assertIsInstance</code> covers the type contract and <code>assertEqual</code> verifies the exact value. <code>assertTrue</code> would pass for any truthy result and does not check either.</p>"}]});
